</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Calorie Burn: Under Desk Elliptical vs Treadmill</h2>
<p>Walking is harder than seated pedaling because your legs hold you up. In the Compendium of Physical Activities, walking at 3 mph is about 3.5 METs, while seated pedaling runs from about 2.0 (light) to 2.8 (moderate). Sitting still is about 1.3.</p>
<p>Desk walking is usually slower than 3 mph, because typing at a brisk pace is hard. At 1.5 to 2 mph you are closer to 2.5 to 3 METs. For a 170 lb (77 kg) person, that is roughly 200 to 240 calories an hour on the treadmill versus about 160 to 225 on a seated elliptical, using the standard formula of MET × 3.5 × kg ÷ 200 per minute.</p>
<p>So the treadmill does burn more, but not by a huge margin at desk speeds. What decides your total is time on the machine. Most people can pedal seated for far longer than they can stand and walk, especially in the first few weeks. Estimate your own numbers with our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">under desk elliptical calorie calculator</a>.</p>
[[tool:calories]]

<h3>What about step counts?</h3>
<p>If your goal is a daily step number, the treadmill has a built-in advantage: your watch or phone counts every step as normal walking. Seated pedaling is often missed by wrist trackers because your arms stay still, so an hour on an elliptical can show up as almost nothing. The fix is to log elliptical sessions as a workout or convert the minutes into an equivalent. Our guide on <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-count-as-steps/">whether an under desk elliptical counts as steps</a> explains how to do that.</p>

<h2>Working While You Move</h2>
<p>A seated elliptical barely affects your typing or mouse control at light resistance, because your upper body stays still. Owners often report that they pedal through entire meetings and writing sessions without noticing.</p>
<p>On a treadmill, your body moves with each step. Calls, email and reading are fine for most people at slow speeds. Detailed work, such as spreadsheets or design, tends to suffer, so many treadmill owners walk for part of the day and sit for focused work. The [[amazon:walkingpad-c2|WalkingPad C2]] and similar slim models are designed for this kind of slow desk walking.</p>
<h3>Noise in shared spaces</h3>
<p>A magnetic seated elliptical is the clear winner for open offices, shared homes and apartments. Treadmill motors are fairly quiet, but footfalls carry through floors and can be picked up on calls. If quiet is a priority, see our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> picks.</p>

<h2>Joints, Comfort and Safety</h2>
<p>With an under desk elliptical, your weight stays on the chair and your feet stay on the pedals. That makes it one of the gentlest ways to move, and a good fit for older adults, people coming back from injury and anyone who finds standing for long periods tiring. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide focuses on stable, easy-to-use models.</p>
<p>A treadmill is still low impact at walking speeds, and standing has its own benefits for posture and leg strength. But you need decent balance, supportive shoes and attention to where your feet are. If you have a heart, joint or balance condition, talk to your doctor before starting either machine.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Safety note:</strong> Do not try to stand on a seated under desk elliptical as a substitute for a treadmill. Most are not built for your full body weight. Read <a href="/can-you-stand-on-an-under-desk-elliptical/">can you stand on an under desk elliptical</a> for the details.</p></div>

<h2>Space, Setup and Cost</h2>
<p>A seated elliptical fits in the footwell of almost any desk. You will want a non-slip mat, and caster cups if your chair has wheels. Check the knee room first with our <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>, because a low desk is the most common problem.</p>
<p>An under desk treadmill needs a clear strip of floor in front of a standing desk, an outlet and somewhere to store it. Folding models help, but they are still heavier and bulkier to move around. If you do not own a standing desk, add that cost to the total.</p>
<p>Over time, the treadmill also needs more care: belt alignment, lubrication and a motor that can wear. A manual elliptical has far fewer parts to fail. Always check the current price and warranty on the listing.</p>
<p>Portability is another quiet advantage of the seated option. A manual elliptical can move from your office to the living room for evening TV in seconds, and many people get as much use from it on the couch as at the desk. A treadmill tends to stay wherever it is set up. If you want something you can carry between rooms easily, see our <a href="/lightweight-under-desk-elliptical/">lightweight under desk elliptical</a> picks.</p>

<h2>What If You Want a Real Workout?</h2>
<p>Neither under desk machine is a replacement for a proper cardio session. If what you really want is a 30-minute sweat before or after work, consider a compact full-size machine instead. The [[amazon:niceday|Niceday 2-in-1 elliptical]] is a standing elliptical with a small footprint, and an under desk treadmill that also folds up for jogging, like the [[amazon:urevo-2in1|UREVO 2-in-1]], gives you more speed than a basic walking pad.</p>
<p>Many people pair the two approaches: light movement under the desk through the day, and a short, harder session on a separate machine or outdoors a few times a week. That combination is the most realistic route to the 150 minutes of moderate activity a week recommended by the CDC and WHO.</p>

<h2>A Typical Workday With Each Machine</h2>
<p>It helps to picture how each option fits into a real day, because that is what decides whether you keep using it.</p>
<h3>With an under desk elliptical</h3>
<p>You sit down at 9, start pedaling at light resistance while you clear email, and keep going through your first call. You pause for focused writing if you need to, then pedal again through the afternoon slump. Many owners settle into 10 to 20 minute blocks spread across the day, adding up to one to three hours without ever feeling like a workout. Nothing changes about your desk, chair or how you look on camera.</p>
<h3>With an under desk treadmill</h3>
<p>You raise the desk, step on and walk at a slow pace during your morning calls and inbox time. After 30 to 60 minutes your feet or focus tell you to stop, so you fold or slide the deck aside, lower the desk and sit for deep work. You may walk again after lunch. The total time on the machine is often shorter, but each minute counts for a bit more.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Whichever you choose, tie it to a trigger you already have, such as "every call" or "every time I open my inbox." Habits anchored to existing routines tend to stick far better than vague goals. A <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> also helps.</p></div>

